From 5e6760be4b703139c07bea56fab2c121960071b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Andrei V. Lepikhov" <lepihov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 20:17:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH v0 02/10] Add a memcmp fast path to numeric_eq

Identical stored representations always denote equal values, so equality --
unlike ordering -- can begin with a memcmp of the two datums and answer true
without unpacking either one.  Values in one column share a display scale and
store the same digits, so this hits for nearly every comparison that hash
aggregation and hash joins perform.  Equal values with differing scales, and
compressed or out-of-line values, fall through to the general case.

The shortcut is valid for equality only.  Ordering cannot use it: 1.5 and 1.50
are equal but differ bytewise, so a non-zero memcmp says nothing about which is
the larger.

This is deliberately a separate commit from the stack-unpacking that precedes
it.  That change moves a buffer; this one rests on an argument about what the
stored representation of a numeric means, so it should be reviewable, and
revertable, on its own.  It is also the larger half of the benefit wherever the
grouping key is wide or the hash table is large.  Measured on top of the
preceding commit, same machine and method: grouping a million rows by twenty
numeric(15,2) columns 2396 -> 1146 ms, grouping ten million rows by one numeric
key over ten thousand groups 1911 -> 1208 ms, EXCEPT 2258 -> 1686 ms, and a
hash join 2621 -> 2092 ms.  Against unpatched code those four are 2745 -> 1146,
2241 -> 1208, 2798 -> 1686 and 3003 -> 2092 ms respectively, so this commit
contributes between two thirds and four fifths of the total.

It also helps precisely where the preceding commit cannot: grouping by a column
of two-hundred-digit numerics, too wide to unpack onto the stack, still went
1584 -> 1130 ms, because the memcmp answers without unpacking at all.

It is not uniformly a win.  Comparing two different columns, as in WHERE a = b,
the operands are almost never bytewise equal, so every call pays for a memcmp
that fails and then does the full comparison anyway: that query measured
401 -> 445 ms here, still well ahead of the 635 ms baseline but giving back a
fifth of what the previous commit won.  Grouping on a single narrow key is
neutral within noise.  The shapes that lose are rarer than the ones that gain,
but the trade is real.

With both commits in place, instructions retired -- counted exactly under
callgrind rather than sampled -- fall 26.3% for a single-column HashAggregate,
37.7% for a four-column one, and 19.2% for WHERE a = b.
---
 src/backend/utils/adt/numeric.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/backend/utils/adt/numeric.c b/src/backend/utils/adt/numeric.c
index 2e574b26d30..897c25e9d7b 100644
--- a/src/backend/utils/adt/numeric.c
+++ b/src/backend/utils/adt/numeric.c
@@ -2525,14 +2525,42 @@ numeric_cmp(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)
 Datum
 numeric_eq(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)
 {
+	struct varlena *att1 = (struct varlena *) PG_GETARG_POINTER(0);
+	struct varlena *att2 = (struct varlena *) PG_GETARG_POINTER(1);
 	NumericLocalBuf buf1;
 	NumericLocalBuf buf2;
 	bool		free1;
 	bool		free2;
-	Numeric		num1 = numeric_unpack_local(PG_GETARG_DATUM(0), &buf1, &free1);
-	Numeric		num2 = numeric_unpack_local(PG_GETARG_DATUM(1), &buf2, &free2);
+	Numeric		num1;
+	Numeric		num2;
 	bool		result;
 
+	/*
+	 * Identical stored representations always denote equal values, so for
+	 * equality -- unlike ordering -- we can start with a memcmp.  Values of
+	 * the same column share a display scale and store the same digits, so this
+	 * hits for nearly every comparison done by hash aggregation and hash
+	 * joins, and answers without unpacking either input.  Values that compare
+	 * equal with different scales, and compressed or out-of-line values, fall
+	 * through to the general case.
+	 *
+	 * Do not be tempted to reuse this for ordering: 1.5 and 1.50 are equal but
+	 * differ bytewise, so a non-zero memcmp implies nothing about which of two
+	 * numerics is the larger.
+	 */
+	if (likely(!VARATT_IS_COMPRESSED(att1) && !VARATT_IS_EXTERNAL(att1) &&
+			   !VARATT_IS_COMPRESSED(att2) && !VARATT_IS_EXTERNAL(att2)))
+	{
+		Size		len = VARSIZE_ANY_EXHDR(att1);
+
+		if (len == VARSIZE_ANY_EXHDR(att2) &&
+			memcmp(VARDATA_ANY(att1), VARDATA_ANY(att2), len) == 0)
+			PG_RETURN_BOOL(true);
+	}
+
+	num1 = numeric_unpack_local(PG_GETARG_DATUM(0), &buf1, &free1);
+	num2 = numeric_unpack_local(PG_GETARG_DATUM(1), &buf2, &free2);
+
 	result = cmp_numerics(num1, num2) == 0;
 
 	if (unlikely(free1))
-- 
